{"tool": "perry-task", "args": ["list", "--json", "--root", "/var/folders/6g/dpvy7sgj7918yj3pqwnvy5q00000gn/T/tmpqzfio7ty", "--all", "--limit", "1"], "exit": 0, "stdout": "{\n  \"contract\": \"perry-task/list/2.4\",\n  \"installed\": true,\n  \"bound\": {\n    \"limit\": 1,\n    \"default\": false,\n    \"returned\": 1,\n    \"total\": 6,\n    \"open_total\": 4,\n    \"closed_total\": 2,\n    \"truncated\": true,\n    \"order\": \"by id, ascending\"\n  },\n  \"semantics\": [\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.5\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"evidence_paths\",\n        \"conformance.evidence_not_found\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"Evidence spans are now resolved for CLOSED rows too, and every span that resolves nowhere is reported. Before 1.5 both arrays were empty on a closed row, so a consumer could not tell 'Perry did not resolve this' from 'this file is missing'.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.7\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"timeline[].from\",\n        \"timeline[].to\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"These were a status transition on every event until `prioritize` shipped, where they are board sections. The new `timeline[].field` key says which, per event. Nothing about the existing events changed \u2014 but a consumer that hardcoded 'from/to is a status' was correct before and is not now.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.9\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"conformance.rows_with_no_computable_age\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"Empty when `conformance.has_event_log` is false. On a project with no event log EVERY open row landed here by construction, so the array restated the flag once per row rather than naming a finding \u2014 17 of 17 on the consumer that reported it. It now reports only rows whose age is uncomputable for a reason the flag does not already give. If you rendered this list, check `has_event_log` instead.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.10\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"status_text\",\n        \"conformance.sections_read\",\n        \"conformance.sections_skipped\",\n        \"conformance.rows_with_unrecognized_id\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"`status_text` is now a legacy display alias of the typed `status` field. It no longer exposes raw or off-enum text from the BOARD.md projection; `status` in tasks.jsonl is the only current status truth. `sections_read` now summarizes stored groups, while `sections_skipped` and `rows_with_unrecognized_id` remain legacy keys and are empty because task reads no longer scan Board tables.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.12\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"startable\",\n        \"blocked_stale\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"A stored `blocked` status no longer masks an empty `blocked_by`. Before 1.12 `status` was read first, so a row whose every declared dependency had closed reported `blocked_by: []` and `startable: false` in the same object and no consumer could see the contradiction \u2014 measured on two of four blocked rows on Perry's own board. Such a row is now `startable: true` and carries the new `blocked_stale: true`. The stored `status` is unchanged and still reads `blocked`: this payload reports, it does not rewrite cells. A row with an open dependency, a `blocked` row that declares no dependency at all, and any row in `review` are all unaffected. If you rendered a `blocked` badge from `status`, render `blocked_stale` beside it \u2014 the two disagree exactly when the board is out of date.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.13\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"conformance.next_action_cites_closed\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"Each entry now carries `means`, `readings`, `row_status` and `blocked_stale`. The existing `id`/`cites`/`status` are unchanged \u2014 what changed is what the array is FOR. A bare `{id, cites, status}` triple reads as a wording complaint, and on 2026-08-20 it was fired on exactly the two rows on this board that were stranded, read as prose hygiene, and silenced by rewriting the cells. It is not a style check: the row citing closed work and the row being unblocked are the same observation, and this array does not decide which. If you rendered these as lint, render `means` instead and route them to whoever can tell the two readings apart.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.14\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"blocked_by\",\n        \"startable\",\n        \"blocked_stale\",\n        \"conformance.depends_on_unknown\",\n        \"conformance.blocked_by_closed_rows\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"A `USER-` ask is now a NODE in the dependency graph. Before 1.14 the only register the graph resolved against was `tasks.jsonl`, so `depends_on USER-015` was accepted by the writer and reported by the reader as `depends_on_unknown` on the same row \u2014 Perry writes that edge itself, from the other end, whenever `ask --blocks` names a task. Now an ask this project has issued resolves, a PENDING one leaves the row in `blocked_by` exactly as an open task does, and an ANSWERED one satisfies the edge exactly as a closed task does. Two consequences a consumer must expect: such a row becomes `startable: true` with `blocked_stale: true` once its ask comes back, and `blocked_by_closed_rows` now names rows whose dependency was answered rather than closed \u2014 'terminal' covers both. Nothing got weaker: an id NEITHER register carries \u2014 a mistyped task number, a `USER-` id the queue never minted, and every `DESIGN-`/`ADR-` handle, none of which have a state Perry can ever read \u2014 is still unknown and still unsatisfied.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.16\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"drift.drift\",\n        \"drift.unrecorded\",\n        \"drift.unrecorded_sample\",\n        \"drift.orphaned\",\n        \"drift.stale_done\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"All five are `null` rather than `0`/`[]` when `drift.checked` is `false`. The page said, until 1.16, that when there is no event log 'everything else is then zero or empty' \u2014 so a consumer reading `drift == 0` as 'no drift' was following the documented text, not ignoring it. It now reads `null`, which rule 1 names as this payload's unknown value, and a consumer that skipped `checked` fails loudly instead of being reassured. Nothing about a project WITH a log changed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"1.18\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"tasks[].created\",\n        \"tasks[].updated\",\n        \"tasks[].timeline[].ts\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"Every event appended from now on stamps its UTC OFFSET: `2026-08-28T02:15:22+08:00`, where the same event used to be written `2026-08-28T02:15:22` and meant local wall clock without saying so. The lines already in the log are not rewritten and still carry no zone; `bin/lib \u00a7 ts_moment` reads those as the reading machine's local time, which is what `datetime.now()` wrote them as. Local rather than UTC on purpose, so the log's text goes on rising across the cutover. A consumer that parses `ts` must accept an offset \u2014 `datetime.fromisoformat` always has; a hand-rolled `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S` will not \u2014 and one that compares two of them as TEXT is why this moved: the register stamps UTC, and comparing the two shapes as strings put every move inside the machine's offset on the wrong side of the assertion it was compared against.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"2.0\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"bound\",\n        \"tasks\",\n        \"open\",\n        \"closed\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"`tasks[]` IS NOW BOUNDED, and this is a MAJOR because a consumer that changes nothing receives fewer rows. A call that names no `--limit` returns at most 200 and `bound.truncated` says so; `bound.total`, `bound.open_total` and `bound.closed_total` are the figures before the bound. Until 2.0 every matching row came back, which on this repository is 538KB for a default call and 1.68MB for `--all` \u2014 about 420k tokens, more than any context window it is read in. `--limit 0` restores the old behaviour explicitly. `open` and `closed` still count THIS payload's rows, so on a truncated call they count the returned ones: measured here, `--all` reported 24 open against the project's 156, which is why the major is the honest number even though no key changed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"2.1\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"asks.items\",\n        \"asks.open\",\n        \"asks.items[].idle\",\n        \"risks.items\",\n        \"risks.open\",\n        \"risks.cleared\",\n        \"risks.source\",\n        \"intake.rows\",\n        \"intake.undischarged\",\n        \"intake.oldest_undischarged\",\n        \"tasks[].depends_on_resolved\",\n        \"tasks[].blocked_by\",\n        \"tasks[].startable\",\n        \"conformance.depends_on_unknown\",\n        \"drift.drift\",\n        \"drift.unrecorded\",\n        \"drift.unrecorded_sample\",\n        \"drift.orphaned\",\n        \"drift.stale_done\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"The asks, risks and intake registers are now READ FROM THEIR STORES \u2014 `asks.jsonl`, `risks.jsonl`, `intake.jsonl` \u2014 and not parsed out of `BOARD.md`, which is read only by a project that has no such store. That includes the ask register the dependency graph resolves a `USER-` edge against. Before 2.1 every field named here was built from the file, so with `BOARD.md` absent they answered wrongly at exit 0. Measured on this repository at b7c89276, with 33 asks and 4 risks in the stores: `asks` empty, `risks.open` 0 with `risks.source` \\\"none\\\", eight answered-ask edges `kind: \\\"unknown\\\"` and unsatisfied, and `drift.drift` 96. With no file on disk `drift` is now computed against the store's rows, since there is no projection to have drifted. On a project whose `BOARD.md` agrees with its stores every value is unchanged except `asks.items[].idle`, which is `\\\"\\\"`: the ask store holds no `Idle` cell, and `idle_days` is derived from `asked` as before. Where a hand-edited `BOARD.md` disagreed with a store, these fields now report the store.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"2.2\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"installed\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"`installed` is added (TASK-237 3b\u2032). Before 2.2, on a directory that is not a Perry project this payload answered its empty shape at exit 0 \u2014 `tasks` empty, `open` 0, `risks`, `asks` and `intake` empty \u2014 exactly as it answers for a project that has nothing in it, so a consumer could not tell the two apart. `installed: false` now says which. The criterion is `schema/README.md \u00a7 installed`: `.perry/config.jsonl` at the project root, or a canonical store under the state root; `BOARD.md`, `OKR.md`, `phase/` or `design/` alone do not count. A consumer that read an empty payload as 'this project has nothing' must read `installed` first.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"2.3\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"installed\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"`installed` is narrower (TASK-237 Amendment (7), user decision 2026-09-14). At 2.2 a canonical store under the state root counted on its own, so a folder holding only a file named `tasks.jsonl` \u2014 another tool's \u2014 read `installed: true`. From 2.3 a store counts only when a `.perry/` directory exists at the project root beside it: `installed` is `.perry/config.jsonl` at the project root, or `.perry/` there and a canonical store under the state root (`schema/README.md \u00a7 installed`). No key moved. A directory holding a store and no `.perry/` now answers `installed: false` with the empty shape at exit 0; every project Perry started writes `.perry/config.jsonl` first and is unaffected.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"version\": \"2.4\",\n      \"fields\": [\n        \"asks.items\",\n        \"asks.open\",\n        \"risks.items\",\n        \"risks.open\",\n        \"risks.cleared\",\n        \"risks.source\",\n        \"intake.rows\",\n        \"intake.undischarged\",\n        \"intake.oldest_undischarged\",\n        \"tasks[].depends_on_resolved\",\n        \"tasks[].blocked_by\",\n        \"tasks[].startable\",\n        \"conformance.depends_on_unknown\",\n        \"conformance.missing_projection\",\n        \"drift.drift\",\n        \"drift.unrecorded\",\n        \"drift.unrecorded_sample\",\n        \"drift.orphaned\",\n        \"drift.stale_done\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"A held `BOARD.md` is no longer read (TASK-262 Amendment (4), round 4b; the user's decision of 2026-09-15). Before 2.4 a project with no `asks.jsonl`, `risks.jsonl` or `intake.jsonl` read that register out of a `BOARD.md` it still held \u2014 the ask register a `USER-` dependency edge resolves against included \u2014 and `drift` was computed against the file's rows. From 2.4 each register is read from its store and nothing else. A project that holds a board and has no store for a register reports that register empty: `asks.items` [], `intake.rows` [], and `risks` from `PROJECT_STATE.md`'s bullets alone (`risks.source` \\\"none\\\" when it has none). A `USER-` edge whose ask exists only in the file is `kind: \\\"unknown\\\"` and unsatisfied, so its row loses `startable` and lands in `depends_on_unknown`. `drift` is computed against the rows `perry-tasks board` prints \u2014 the store's \u2014 on every project, which is what a board-less project was already told at 2.1; where a held file disagreed with its stores the values move from the file's to the store's. `conformance.missing_projection` keeps its value (`\\\"\\\"` when a `BOARD.md` exists at the state root, else its path) and is now an existence probe only: no reader opens the file. No key was added, removed or retyped. A consumer of a store-less, board-holding project must run the `--from-board` imports (`reference/version-compatibility.md \u00a7 Upgrading a project to G3`); after them nothing here differs from what the file said.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"risks\": {\n    \"items\": [],\n    \"open\": 0,\n    \"cleared\": 0,\n    \"source\": \"none\"\n  },\n  \"asks\": {\n    \"items\": [],\n    \"open\": 0\n  },\n  \"drift\": {\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"baseline\": \"2026-09-17T14:33:57+08:00\",\n    \"drift\": 0,\n    \"unrecorded\": 0,\n    \"unrecorded_sample\": [],\n    \"orphaned\": [],\n    \"stale_done\": []\n  },\n  \"intake\": {\n    \"rows\": [],\n    \"undischarged\": 0,\n    \"oldest_undischarged\": null\n  },\n  \"project_root\": \"/private/var/folders/6g/dpvy7sgj7918yj3pqwnvy5q00000gn/T/tmpqzfio7ty\",\n  \"state_root\": \"/private/var/folders/6g/dpvy7sgj7918yj3pqwnvy5q00000gn/T/tmpqzfio7ty\",\n  \"conformance\": {\n    \"sections_read\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"P1\",\n        \"priority\": \"P1\",\n        \"rows\": 6\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sections_skipped\": [],\n    \"rows_with_unrecognized_id\": [],\n    \"off_enum_status\": [],\n    \"rows_with_no_status\": [],\n    \"evidence_not_found\": [],\n    \"rows_with_no_computable_age\": [],\n    \"next_action_cites_closed\": [],\n    \"depends_on_unknown\": [],\n    \"dependency_cycles\": [],\n    \"blocked_without_dependency\": [\n      \"TASK-003\"\n    ],\n    \"blocked_by_closed_rows\": [],\n    \"in_progress_with_no_live_run\": [],\n    \"review_idle\": [],\n    \"has_event_log\": true,\n    \"missing_projection\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"tasks\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"TASK-001\",\n      \"title\": \"independent row 0\",\n      \"summary\": \"A fixture row that exists so the writer has something to write. It carries no meaning beyond that.\",\n      \"owner\": \"Coding Agent\",\n      \"priority\": \"P1\",\n      \"status\": \"done\",\n      \"status_text\": \"done\",\n      \"track\": \"main\",\n      \"mode\": \"project\",\n      \"stage\": \"\",\n      \"stage_since\": \"\",\n      \"arrived\": \"\",\n      \"parent\": \"\",\n      \"commitment\": \"\",\n      \"next_action\": \"\u2014\",\n      \"evidence\": \"evidence.md\",\n      \"evidence_paths\": [\n        \"evidence.md\"\n      ],\n      \"evidence_relations\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"evidence.md\",\n          \"path\": \"evidence.md\",\n          \"kind\": \"file\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"verification\": \"V3\",\n      \"open\": false,\n      \"group\": \"P1\",\n      \"role\": \"\",\n      \"depends_on\": [],\n      \"depends_on_resolved\": [],\n      \"blocked_by\": [],\n      \"blocks\": [],\n      \"startable\": false,\n      \"blocked_stale\": false,\n      \"created\": \"2026-09-17T14:33:57+08:00\",\n      \"updated\": \"2026-09-17T14:33:57+08:00\",\n      \"timeline\": [\n        {\n          \"ts\": \"2026-09-17T14:33:57+08:00\",\n          \"event\": \"add\",\n          \"from\": null,\n          \"to\": \"not_started\",\n          \"actor\": \"task_writer_support\",\n          \"field\": \"status\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"ts\": \"2026-09-17T14:33:57+08:00\",\n          \"event\": \"done\",\n          \"from\": \"not_started\",\n          \"to\": \"done\",\n          \"actor\": \"task_writer_support\",\n          \"field\": \"status\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"open\": 0,\n  \"closed\": 1,\n  \"events\": 11,\n  \"untitled\": []\n}\n", "stderr": "perry-task: list returned 1 of 6 row(s) \u2014 the --limit you passed. `open` and `closed` count the returned rows; `bound.open_total` is 4. Pass `--limit 0` for every row, `--limit <n>` for a different one, or `--track <name>` to narrow the set.\n"}
